TS 26.04 on Wayland - X11 display driver, Multi-window, 2 monitors (1920x1080 @ 143.88 Hz, 1920x1080 @ 59.88 Hz) - Vulkan (Forward+) - dedicated AMD Radeon RX 6600 (RADV NAVI23) - Intel(R) Core(TM) i7-6700 CPU @ 3.40GHz (8 threads) - 31.29 GiB memory - PulseAudio (44100 Hz, Stereo/mono)\n\n### Issue description\n\nWhile trying out the new Trail3D as a trail for a sword slash, I noticed sometimes the final segment of the trail would stay visible.  Maybe I'm using it wrong, but I would expect since `limit_mode` is set to Time that once `emitting` is turned off that the remaining trail would gradually disappear (and most of the time it does).\n\nEDIT: Lowering `min_section_length` seems to fix it, but should that be necessary when removing sections, not adding them?\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nI managed to reproduce it in an MRP, but it seems to depend on my monitor's refresh rate.  It reproduces extremely reliably on my 144Hz monitor, but on my 60Hz I can't get it to happen at all.\n\n1. Run lingering_trail.tscn on a high refresh rate monitor.\n2. Click \"Swing\"\n\nUsually looks like this on 144Hz:\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/4dbeab12-3fdd-4010-b382-e5e18dbd9e94\n\nBut works fine on 60Hz:\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/8e34eece-5b27-432e-8deb-34219a6f1167\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\n[lingering-trail.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/31645199/lingering-trail.zip)","closed_by":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/123031/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/123031/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null,"pinned_comment":null}